## How the online upgrade works

An upgrade doesn't modify the instance that serves your traffic. Appwrite provisions a second instance on PostgreSQL 19, streams your data into it with logical replication, and waits for replication to catch up. Reads continue on the current instance the whole time.

At the cutover, the old instance is fenced read-only and existing client connections are closed. Writes pause for that moment, and your application reconnects to the database, now on PostgreSQL 19. A connection pool that retries dropped connections handles this without code changes. Because connections close, start the upgrade during a low-traffic period.

The target version must be newer than the current one, and an upgrade can't be reversed.

# What's new in PostgreSQL 19

PostgreSQL 19 adds new SQL for get-or-create writes, an online way to reclaim table bloat, and faster foreign key checks, alongside planner, replication, and monitoring work. The [PostgreSQL 19 release notes](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/release/19.0/) cover all of it. These are the changes application developers are likely to notice first.

## Get-or-create with ON CONFLICT DO SELECT

`INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` skips a conflicting row and returns nothing for it. Get-or-create code usually follows it with a second `SELECT`, or uses a no-op `DO UPDATE` that writes a new row version just to get the row back. PostgreSQL 19 adds `DO SELECT`, which returns the existing row through `RETURNING`:

```sql
INSERT INTO tags (slug, name)
VALUES ('postgres', 'Postgres')
ON CONFLICT (slug) DO SELECT
RETURNING id, slug, name;
```

One statement returns the row whether it was just inserted or already existed. `DO SELECT` requires a conflict target and a `RETURNING` clause, and `DO SELECT FOR UPDATE` also locks the existing row against concurrent updates.

## Online table repacking with REPACK

Tables with heavy updates and deletes accumulate dead space. Autovacuum marks that space for reuse, but it doesn't shrink the table. Reclaiming it used to mean `VACUUM FULL` or `CLUSTER`, which lock the table while they run.

PostgreSQL 19 adds the `REPACK` command. `REPACK (CONCURRENTLY)` rewrites a table while reads and writes continue, and takes an exclusive lock only for its final phase. The concurrent mode needs a primary key or an index-based replica identity on the table, and it can't run inside a transaction block. `VACUUM FULL` and `CLUSTER` remain available.

## Faster bulk inserts with foreign keys

PostgreSQL 19 speeds up foreign key checks, which in the PostgreSQL project's testing made bulk inserts nearly 2x faster. There is nothing to turn on. Imports and batch writes into tables with foreign keys benefit as soon as the database runs PostgreSQL 19.

## Behavior changes to check before you upgrade

A few behaviors changed in ways application code can notice:

- **`json_array()` over zero rows** now returns an empty JSON array instead of `NULL`. Code that checks for `NULL` there needs updating.
- **`standard_conforming_strings`** can no longer be turned off, so backslashes in ordinary string literals are always literal characters. Use `E'...'` strings for escape sequences.
- **MD5 passwords** now produce a warning after each successful login, and MD5 support will be removed in a future PostgreSQL release. Move roles that use MD5 to SCRAM-SHA-256 passwords.

The [maintenance docs](/docs/products/databases/postgresql/maintenance#postgresql-19) keep this list next to the upgrade steps.

# When to move to PostgreSQL 19

The right time depends on where your database is today:

- **New projects.** Create the database and you are on PostgreSQL 19. Start on an older version only if a dependency requires it.
- **Existing databases on 17 or 18.** Create a new PostgreSQL 19 database, run your migrations and test suite against it, then upgrade production. Take a [manual backup](/docs/products/databases/postgresql/backups#manual) right before you start, so you have a recent restore point.
- **Databases that depend on a specific extension.** Check that each installed [extension](/docs/products/databases/postgresql/extensions) supports PostgreSQL 19 before you upgrade. pgvector, PostGIS, and pg_trgm are already in the PostgreSQL 19 extension catalog. If one you need isn't yet, stay on your current version. Nothing changes until you start an upgrade.

Upgrades are one-way, so test first. Once your application passes against PostgreSQL 19, the upgrade itself is a single click or API call.

# Get started with PostgreSQL 19 on Appwrite

PostgreSQL 19 is available on Appwrite Cloud today. Create a new database to start on it, or open **Settings** > **General** on an existing one to upgrade. If you are new to managed PostgreSQL on Appwrite, the [launch post](/blog/post/appwrite-now-speaks-postgresql) covers connections, backups, branches, and high availability.

